<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Okay so I generally do not read drafts for SCPs that people are asking me to greenlight, but I was in a charitable mood and I had a skim through yours; or specifically, I skipped to the addendum because I figured you have summarised the ConProcs/Description well enough here that I don't need to read them again.</p>
<p>The core issue is it's not a cohesive story: Addendum 1 tells us where it was found; Addendum 2 gives some random additional detail that should be in the description; Addendum 3 gives a dry retelling of it escaping during a CI raid; and then Addendum 4 tells us even more about what the SCP can do.</p>
<p>And like&#8230;none of these addendums build off what came before? Addendum 1 has to be the first one but the other three could be shuffled around freely into any order without significantly changing their impact.</p>
<p>This is followed by a lengthy description of the North Koreans attempting to neutralise it with no justification for why the Foundation know this, or any kind of temporal context to establish whether this is like, pre-discovery stuff or post it escaping stuff or anything.</p>
<p>Which brings me back to my core issue with this: the actual narrative is just, extremely weak and really needs work to let you figure out how to do something interesting with it.</p>
<p>The idea of this being a hive-mind made of individual cells with their own needs and wants, but coagulated together to make a humanoid is actually a damn good idea; but it needs to actually be explored: none of the narrative is in any way affected by it being individual cells beyond it being vaguely pathogenic and it making it harder to eradicate this thing.</p>
<p>There's like, a core of a good idea in here but it just, really needs time and effort to polish it into a state which works; and it really needs you to strongly consider how you can like, actually tell the story in a way that makes sense for the Foundation to know about; and to like, actually explore what the anomaly does in an interesting way.</p>
<p>It also really feels like the core needs stripped right down: at the moment, the anomaly is an overcomplex mess with like, four different 'processes' that don't need to be formal things and can just be summarised as little things it can do in an informal manner.</p>
<p>To summarise: reading the draft has more strongly convinced me that you need to put this on the back burner and read a bunch of modern SCPs to really get a feel for <em>how</em> SCPs tell stories and how you can get a reader invested in the characters present, and the events listed within.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

